WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.260
Dependencies are part of the product, not an implementation footnote.

2
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:16.920
A C++ project may locate system packages, vendor a library, use submodules, call a package manager, use FetchContent, use ExternalProject, or link to prebuilt binaries.

3
00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:25.660
Each approach has different implications for trust, reproducibility, licensing, update cadence, and the ability to inspect what was included.

4
00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:29.700
Release workflows should make dependency selection explicit.

5
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:36.360
Engineers should be able to tell which version was used, where it came from, how it was verified, and why that source is approved.

6
00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:40.060
When practical, versions and source locations should be pinned.

7
00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:47.600
Integrity expectations, such as checksums or package manager lock data, should be part of the normal build record rather than a separate tribal memory.

8
00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.560
Remote fetching during ordinary builds deserves strict control.

9
00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:59.820
It can be convenient during prototyping, but release builds should not quietly depend on whatever is reachable from a developer workstation at configuration time.

10
00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:12.320
Many organizations prefer a curated mirror, an internal package registry, a reviewed vendor directory, or CI preparation steps that make dependency inputs stable before CMake configures the project.

11
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.200
Dependency updates should follow an engineering process.

12
00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:23.820
A version change can affect ABI behavior, compiler settings, transitive libraries, package contents, or platform support.

13
00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:29.040
Secure CMake practice makes those changes visible, testable, and reviewable.

14
00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:38.020
The build should log the selected dependency path or package identity clearly enough that CI and release reviewers can confirm the expected component was used.
